C = [
("h1", "8. The Fulness Dwelling Bodily"),
("p", "Claim: Colossians 1:19 and 2:9 say that all the fulness dwells in Christ, and the second says that it dwells bodily and names the fulness “of the Godhead.” The two sentences are read together, and the second is the plainest statement in Paul that the one who was in a body was fully God."),
("sub", "8.1 The two verses"),
("p", "The first verse is “For it pleased the Father that in him should all fulness dwell” (Colossians 1:19). The second is “For in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily” (Colossians 2:9). Both use the verb dwell, and both speak of fulness. The first names the Father as the one whose pleasure it was. The second adds the Godhead as the content of the fulness and adds the adverb “bodily.” The first stands in the passage on creation and the church. The second stands at the centre of the argument against the rival teachers."),
("sub", "8.2 Why the second verse is placed where it is"),
("p", "Paul sets the verse in the middle of his warning. He has said, “Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit” (Colossians 2:8), and he gives the reason: “For in him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily” (Colossians 2:9). Then he adds, “And ye are complete in him, which is the head of all principality and power” (Colossians 2:10). The argument is that nothing is lacking in Christ, and that those who are in him are complete. A teacher who offers a fulness beside Christ is offering a thing that is already there."),
("sub", "8.3 “All the fulness” and “the Godhead”"),
("p", "“All” is a strong word. The fulness is not partly in him and partly elsewhere. “Godhead” is the word the King James Version uses for deity, and the sentence does not use a title in place of the thing. Thomas Constable states it directly: “The fullness of the Godhead is in Christ. This is an eternal fact that is always true.” John Piper reads it with the other verse: “He is fully God.” The volume agrees with both statements as readings of the sentence. The word “dwelleth” is in the present tense in English and Paul does not limit it."),
("sub", "8.4 “Bodily”"),
("p", "The adverb is the weight of the verse. The fulness dwells in him bodily. The word cannot be read as though the fulness were in a spirit alone, or in a teaching, or in a symbol. It is in one who has a body. Wright writes that “bodily” must not be underestimated in this verse, and the volume agrees with the caution. Philippians 3:21 speaks of “his glorious body,” and 1 Timothy 2:5 of “the man Christ Jesus.” Colossians 2:9 puts the two in one sentence: fulness of the Godhead, and bodily."),
("p", "The verse does not say how the fulness of the Godhead can dwell in a body. It does not say that the body is itself divine, or that it ceased to be a body, or that the Godhead was confined by it. It states the fact. The volume reports it and does not add."),
("sub", "8.5 Not a question of reconciliation"),
("p", "Colossians 1:20 goes on to say that by him all things are reconciled, and that is a statement about the Cross. The volume on the Cross reads it, and the study on reconciliation reads Colossians 1:19 to 23. The point here is only the identity: the one in whom the fulness dwells is the one who made peace through the blood of his cross. Paul does not separate them."),
("ctbl", "The fulness in Colossians.", [
    ("The verse", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("Colossians 1:19", "All fulness dwells in him, by the Father’s pleasure", "What the fulness is; the verse does not name it"),
    ("Colossians 2:9", "All the fulness of the Godhead dwells in him bodily", "How the Godhead dwells in a body"),
    ("Colossians 2:10", "Those in him are complete; he is head of all principality and power", "How completeness in him relates to his headship"),
    ("Colossians 2:3", "In him are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge", "From whom they are hid; the verse says in him"),
  ], [0.20, 0.42, 0.38]),
("sub", "8.6 “Complete in him”"),
("p", "The verse that follows the statement of the fulness gives its use: “And ye are complete in him, which is the head of all principality and power” (Colossians 2:10). The word “complete” and the word “fulness” are of one family in the Greek, and the English hides it. The Colossians are filled in him because the fulness is in him. Earlier in the chapter Paul had said that his purpose was “That their hearts might be comforted, being knit together in love, and unto all riches of the full assurance of understanding, to the acknowledgement of the mystery of God, and of the Father, and of Christ; In whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge” (Colossians 2:2, 3). The three statements fit together. The treasures are in him. The fulness is in him. The readers are complete in him. A rival teacher who offers another source has to deny one of the three."),
("sub", "8.7 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "The fulness dwells in him “all” and “bodily”; neither word is softened.",
    "The content of the fulness in 2:9 is “the Godhead,” and the sentence is present-tense.",
    "The verse is set against rival teachers who offer a fulness beside Christ.",
    "It does not say how the Godhead dwells in a body, and it does not say the body is divine in itself.",
  ]),
("p", "Colossians has now given three sayings: the image and firstborn, the Creator, and the fulness. The next three chapters take the sayings in other letters, beginning with a sentence in Romans."),
]
